{"kind":"report","text":"Checker review of the fixed-slot equality index recut at head f2ae898058587c87b573e7408c4700dbd1ec211a, tree bb685f2b, for request 121a8913 and review request f0254126. Verdict: APPROVED.\n\nThe head adds two commits to the reviewed head 2d6811db. I read the implementation note's repair sections first, then the diff from 2d6811db, against items 1 and 2 of review 9b6930b0. The production change is in `fixed-index.ts`, `fold.ts` and `state.ts` in derive, and in `core.ts`, `sqlite.ts` and `store.ts` in scope. The rest of the head is unchanged, and the findings of 9b6930b0 on the index rule, its equivalence to the scan, replay and the witnesses stand.\n\n## Items of 9b6930b0\n\n1. The readiness marker. Resolved.\n   - `finishFixed` now writes `{v:2, definition, at, head}`, and `applyEntry` passes the entry's seq and hash (`fold.ts:293`). `MemoryState` keys readiness the same way.\n   - `ensureFixed` validates the scope reference and the head. It rebuilds unless the marker matches the actual `scope().head`. `fixedReady` reads at most 4,096 bytes, parses strictly and requires exactly four keys. A v1 marker or any malformed marker reads as not ready.\n   - A base-code fold advances the scope head without touching the marker, so the next `pinned()` rebuilds.\n   - The new native case \"rollforward rebuilds a formerly ready index after a base writer advances the actual scope head and items\" proves it. The base writer leaves the marker byte-identical. After the rebuild, `judgeGuard` answers `fail` for the duplicate, the count includes the base writer's item, and the checkpoint digest is unchanged.\n   - The control replaces only the head comparison with `true`. The duplicate guard then answers `pass`, and the unchanged assertion at `fixed-index.test.ts:149` fails with an ordinary AssertionError. The control restores the source.\n2. The legacy rebuild cost. Resolved.\n   - The generic `rebuildFixed` is gone. The SQLite rebuild pages `SELECT seq,hash FROM entry` in batches of 1,024 rows and parses no entry payload.\n   - It checks that the held positions equal the `entry` rows and that the head hash matches. It then pages items through the type and state index, only for types that declare a fixed slot. A definition with no fixed slot writes only the marker.\n   - The new native case builds 34,000 journal rows with 63,364,507 payload bytes and 2,000 items. The rebuild reads 34,000 metadata rows in 34 batches, and its only journal query is the `seq,hash` page. The counts and the checkpoint digest are correct. The note gives a 98-millisecond round trip, labeled as a direct SQLite fixture that bypasses admission. The note claims no Room A admission and no worst-case bound.\n   - A failed row write rolls back the rebuild, and the marker stays not ready.\n\n## The gate\n\n- Gate 2 at source I stays a pass for that source. Gate 1 stays failed.\n- The repair needed one further full gate under ruling 31962e1f. That gate, at source J 73528f2d with tree cb97eefc, passes all five stages. Its receipt has SHA-256 a35995e7, exit 0, and the same head and tree before and after. It again passes the 2,000-member room and the three default replays.\n- J to the head changes only the note and the manifest, which the documents-only rule in `docs/testing.md` covers.\n\n## Checks at this head\n\n- The worktree is clean. `git merge-tree --write-tree` onto main 4c333072 gives the head tree bb685f2b.\n- I reran the changed owning files once each, with no whole suite:\n  - `security-entry-points.test.mjs`: 10 of 10;\n  - `fixed-index.test.ts`: 5 of 5;\n  - `view.test.ts`: 4 of 4.\n- The retained `native-fixed-repair-1` report covers `fixed-index.test.ts` and `directory-partition-index.test.ts`, with 6 of 6 passed. That run is the source of the note's \"six native cases\".\n- The builder worktree stayed clean after the runs.\n- The manifest has SHA-256 00ff0638 and 45,523 bytes. It follows ruling 4e199bd8 for both roots:\n  - repair root: all